## Objective

Own the complete public UX for app-server-backed Codex sessions. Build on T002's internal launch/attach primitive so a user gets a normal interactive Codex frontend, a generated c2c identity by default, predictable new/resume behavior, and app-server shutdown when the TUI exits.

## Scope and non-goals

- This task owns all public command parsing, help/completion, generated-alias handshake, `--yolo`, saved-instance metadata, lifecycle, and compatibility errors.
- T002 owns only the internal app-server/remote-TUI primitive and security boundary; do not duplicate that transport implementation here.
- T007 owns message-triggered turns. B127 owns durable delivery to a known offline alias; only the offline acceptance criterion below depends on B127.

## Canonical grammar

- `c2c start codex [--alias <alias>] [--yolo] [codex-options...]`: canonical managed entry point; resume the saved thread only when existing `start` semantics explicitly select that instance.
- `c2c codex ...`: exact shortcut for `c2c start codex ...`, with identical parsing, exit codes, help linkage, and no divergent defaults.
- `c2c new codex [--alias <alias>] [--yolo] [codex-options...]`: always create a new Codex thread and instance identity; never silently resume.
- `c2c resume codex <alias> [--yolo] [codex-options...]`: resume the thread saved for that c2c alias. Provide an explicit `--thread-id <id>` escape hatch if the Codex API requires exact selection, with conflicts rejected rather than guessed.

