## State and ownership boundaries

- Codex thread/session ID is authoritative for resume. Persist its mapping to c2c instance identity atomically; never infer resume from a stale PID or display alias alone.
- Derive the default human-readable alias deterministically from the stable Codex/app-server session ID only after that ID is known. The handshake may start with an internal provisional identity, but it must not become externally discoverable or accept mail under the wrong alias.
- Collision handling must be deterministic and stable across restart/resume (for example, extend the session-ID-derived entropy); never select an unrelated random alias on collision.
- Explicit `--alias` overrides display/routing identity but does not replace the authoritative Codex thread ID. Reject conflicts with a live or differently-owned saved alias.
- The owning `c2c` process supervises app-server plus TUI and cleans up endpoint/process metadata on normal exit, signals, and partial startup failure.

## Acceptance criteria

- All four command forms above work and share one implementation path; parser tests reject ambiguous client/alias/thread combinations.
- With no `--alias`, two new threads receive distinct, session-ID-derived aliases; resume/restart retains the same alias. Tests use fixed IDs and prove deterministic collision extension.
- `--alias` is optional, documented as an override, and never inserted into examples as though required.
- `--yolo` prints a conspicuous warning and forwards exactly Codex's `--dangerously-bypass-approvals-and-sandbox`; without it, existing approval and sandbox defaults are unchanged. It is never persisted implicitly into later resumes.
- The stock `codex --remote` interactive TUI owns the foreground terminal. Normal TUI exit, signal, attach failure, and app-server failure terminate/clean up the owned process group without orphaning the server or socket.
- Saved instance/status data distinguishes starting, online/attached, offline, and failed startup; help, shell completions, `c2c instances`, stop/restart, and resume use the same terminology.
- Unsupported Codex versions/capability negotiation fail before publishing a routable alias and give an actionable minimum-version/fallback message.
- Once B127 is complete, exiting the TUI leaves a known offline alias whose sends persist and return success with `delivery=queued_offline`; an unknown alias remains an error. All other criteria can be completed independently of B127.

## Deliverables and receipts

- Focused CLI/lifecycle tests, updated help/completion surfaces, instance schema/migration if required, and an implementation receipt with process-tree cleanup proof and exact command transcripts.
- Document any public grammar incompatibility as a blocker before changing the grammar; do not invent another task ID.

## Verification

```sh
just build
dune exec --root "$PWD" ocaml/test/test_c2c_start.exe
just check
```

Run focused completion/instances tests discovered in the slice and a real tmux launch/exit smoke via `scripts/c2c_tmux.py`; never launch the interactive client ad hoc outside tmux.

## Blockers

P1.M1.E1.T002 must expose the validated internal primitive. B127 blocks only the `queued_offline` acceptance criterion, not grammar, identity, `--yolo`, lifecycle, or online resume work.
